{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "8",
    "case_summary": "A 55-year-old man underwent right hemicolectomy for moderately differentiated adenocarcinoma of the ascending colon, staged as IIIB (T4N1M0). He had no thrombophilic disorders. Preoperative carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) and CA19-9 levels were normal. He completed six months of adjuvant chemotherapy. Thirteen months after surgery, follow-up CT revealed a 25 mm lesion in segment 8 of the liver.  \nOn readmission, hepatitis B and C serologies were negative. Tumor markers including CEA, CA19-9, α-fetoprotein, and PIVKA-II were all within normal limits.  \nCTA demonstrated a liver tumor with two components: a solid portion that enhanced in the early phase and washed out in the delayed phase, and a cystic component. CTAP showed absence of the right portal vein and a perfusion defect throughout the right lobe, suggestive of portal vein tumor thrombus.  \nEndoscopic retrograde cholangiography showed no communication with the biliary tree, and bile cytology was negative for malignancy.  \nFor preoperative diagnosis, an echo-guided biopsy was performed.",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Liver Mass Syndrome with Possible Metastatic Aetiology",
